  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nCurrently-Today... KMLB radar imagery currently shows scattered \nshowers with embedded lightning. GOES-19 satellite imagery and \nlocal observations indicate partly to mostly cloudy skies across \neast central Florida. Temperatures are currently in the mid 70s to\nlow 80s and dew points in the mid 70s to around 80F degrees. \nAnalysis charts show a mid/upper level high (591mb) over the \nsouthwest Atlantic. \n\nRain chances (30-60% chance) increase into the afternoon/evening \nhours (highest rain chances between 2 PM and 8PM) with onshore low \nlevel flow in place coupled with daytime heating. Guidance shows \nMLCAPE up to 1,500-3,200 J/kg, 0-6km shear up to 5-20kts, PWAT \nvalues up to 2.0-2.4\", and 500mb temperatures at -6C. Relatively \nwarm temperatures at 500mb and cloud cover is likely to limit the \nstrong storm potential, however there is the potential for a strong \nstorm or two capable of occasional to frequent lighting, wind \ngusts up to 35-45mph, as well as heavy rainfall (up to 1-3\" in 60 \nminutes). Afternoon highs in the mid 80s to around 90F degrees are\nexpected with heat index values up to 98F-105F degrees. There is \na Moderate HeatRisk which affects anyone sensitive to heat without\nadequate cooling and hydration. Remember to practice heat safety \nif you are sensitive to heat! \n\nSunday-Monday...  A similar forecast is expected to end the weekend \nwith onshore low level flow in place. Rain chances then increase \nacross east central Florida on Monday as a cold front moves \nsoutheast across the Deep South and northern Florida. Expect \nisolated to numerous showers and lightning storms to develop on \nSunday with the highest rain chances in the afternoon and evening \n(mainly between 2PM-8PM). Scattered to widespread showers and \nlightning storms are forecast on Monday with the highest rain\nchances (60-80%) in the afternoon and evening hours. Guidance\ncontinues to show a limited potential for strong storms with 500mb\ntemperatures at around -5C to -4C and moderate MUCAPE values. \nThus, the primary hazards with isolated strong storms will be \noccasional to frequent lightning, wind gusts up to 35-45mph, and \nheavy rainfall (up to 1-3\"+ in 60 minutes). Additionally, the WPC \nhas outlooked northern east central Florida on Monday in a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 out 5) for excessive rainfall. Expect \nafternoon highs in the upper 80s to low 90s with heat index values\nup to 98F to 105F degrees.\n\nTuesday-Friday... Deterministic models and LREF-clusters are in\ngood agreement that a longwave trough will deepen over the\nsoutheastern US through at least Wednesday. At the surface, a wet\nweek is expected as a cold front - which is forecast to extend \nfrom low pressure over the southwest Atlantic - stalls across east\ncentral Florida through the week. The aforementioned front is \nforecast to meander between north central and south central \nFlorida which will result in a tricky forecast for where the \nheaviest rainfall, as well as the greatest flooding potential will\nbe each day. Either way, high rain chances (80-90%) are forecast \nwhich are forecast to gradually reduce mid to late week to 60-70% \nby Friday. The ECMWF and NAEFS ensembles indicate PWAT values \n(2.0\"+) towards the 97.5th+ percentile on Tuesday and Wednesday. \nThe WPC has outlooked much of east central Florida on Tuesday in a\nMarginal Risk (level 1 out 5) for excessive rainfall. \nDeterministic models show relatively warm 500mb temperatures \ncoupled with moderate MUCAPE values and substantial cloud cover. \nTherefore, the main concerns at this time for east central Florida\nwill be showers and lightning storms capable of heavy rainfall \nwhich will have the potential to result in flooding of roadways \nand low- lying areas. Temperatures will be dependent on the \nposition of the stationary boundary with seasonable to below \nnormal temperatures forecast.",
